{"model": "/mnt/sda/models/Qwen3.8-27B", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are given an internal operations dossier.\n\nSection 1. The quarterly logistics review covers warehouse throughput, inbound freight scheduling, and inventory turnover across the regional distribution centres. Each centre reports its dock utilisation and cold-chain compliance.\n\nSection 2. Supplier audits are conducted twice per year. Findings are logged in the compliance register and tracked to closure with an owner and a due date. Repeat findings escalate to the regional operations committee.\n\nSection 3. The maintenance backlog is prioritised by safety impact first, then by production criticality, then by cost. Work orders older than ninety days are reviewed individually by the plant engineering lead.\n\nSection 4. Demand planning uses a rolling thirteen-week horizon. Forecast error is tracked weekly and the bias is reviewed monthly so that safety stock targets can be adjusted before the seasonal peak.\n\nSection 5. The quarterly logistics review covers warehouse throughput, inbound freight scheduling, and inventory turnover across the regional distribution centres. Each centre reports its dock utilisation and cold-chain compliance.\n\nSection 6. Supplier audits are conducted twice per year. Findings are logged in the compliance register and tracked to closure with an owner and a due date. Repeat findings escalate to the regional operations committee.\n\nSection 7. The maintenance backlog is prioritised by safety impact first, then by production criticality, then by cost. Work orders older than ninety days are reviewed individually by the plant engineering lead.\n\nSection 8. Demand planning uses a rolling thirteen-week horizon. Forecast error is tracked weekly and the bias is reviewed monthly so that safety stock targets can be adjusted before the seasonal peak.\n\nSection 9. The quarterly logistics review covers warehouse throughput, inbound freight scheduling, and inventory